d.h1("ABSTRACT", bm="ms_abs")
d.p(f"Microalgae are often proposed as a second feedstock for biodiesel mandates that rely on a single crop. We re-assess a plant designed to grow *Chlorella vulgaris* on flue gas from an 18 MW bagasse power station in Negros Occidental, Philippines, whose earlier evaluations found it profitable. One process model drives a bottom-up techno-economic assessment and an attributional life cycle assessment on ecoinvent 3.12 for two routes, hydrothermal liquefaction with hydrotreating (HTL) and lipid extraction with transesterification (FAME). Recomputing the inherited balance shows that almost all of its algae were an unpaid input; growing them at 20 g/m²/d, near the upper end of measured outdoor productivity, requires about {f0(CORE['area80'])} ha of ponds rather than 10.4 ha to deliver the same 24,000 t/yr to conversion. For a corrected 400 ha plant the minimum fuel selling price is {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD per litre diesel-equivalent for HTL and {f2(F['mfsp_ldeq'])} for FAME, against {f2(CME)} for coconut methyl ester (CME), used here as an economic benchmark (mandate eligibility of HTL fuel is not assumed). Life-cycle emissions ({f1(H['gwp'])} and {f1(F['gwp'])} g CO₂-eq/MJ) give negligible (HTL) or small (FAME, {pct(F['saving'])}) savings against the 94 g/MJ fossil comparator because of grid electricity. Across 60 scenarios, {NMC:,} correlated Monte Carlo draws, Sobol' analysis, stochastic cash flows and NSGA-II optimisation, no evaluated 2026 design reaches the CME benchmark; break-even requires {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} g/m²/d at 400 ha. With independent inputs, the first-order Sobol' indices of productivity and biocrude yield sum to {pct(h0[0][1]+h0[1][1])} of the variance of the cost. With grid decarbonisation, capital learning and biological progress the HTL cost falls to {f2(S['S54']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L diesel-equivalent by 2050; conditional on that pathway, the modelled probability of a cost below the fixed CME benchmark is {pct(r54['p_lt_cme'])}. A neural-network surrogate reproduces the engine's cost and climate results with mean absolute percentage errors of {pct(mh['mfsp_ldeq']['mape'],1)} and {pct(mh['gwp']['mape'],1)} and drives an interactive calculator.")
d.h1("Highlights")
d.bullets([f"Recomputed microalgae plant balance: {NVFAIL} of {NVER} checks fail; algae were fed, not grown",
           f"Corrected flue-gas HTL fuel costs {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L diesel-eq., twice the CME price",
           f"On the Visayas grid the HTL fuel gives negligible GHG savings; a biomass PPA would halve its GHG intensity",
           f"With a net-zero grid and learning, modelled HTL cost falls below CME from {XOVER[('HTL','NZ')]}",
           f"A neural surrogate (R² {mh['mfsp_ldeq']['r2']:.3f}) reproduces the model in a web calculator"])
d.fig("GA_graphical_abstract", w=600, label="Graphical abstract", notitle=True)
d.add("small", [{"t": "Keywords: ", "i": True}, "microalgae; hydrothermal liquefaction; biodiesel mandate; techno-economic assessment; life cycle assessment; global sensitivity analysis; multi-objective optimisation; surrogate model; Philippines"])
d.h1("1. Introduction", bm="ms_intro")
d.p("Biodiesel mandates are among the most common biofuel policies, and their cost depends on the feedstock domestic producers can use. The Philippine Biofuels Act of 2006 requires all diesel to contain a minimum share of biodiesel [@ra9367]. The Act defines biodiesel as FAME or another mono-alkyl ester from vegetable oil or animal fats, approved by the Department of Energy and meeting Philippine National Standards. The mandate is met almost entirely with coconut methyl ester (CME), so its cost moves with the coconut oil market: a scheduled step from 3% to 4% was suspended in 2025, and in August 2026 the move to 5% was deferred because CME cost about PHP 135 per litre against PHP 77–97.5 for diesel [@gma2025,mb2026].")
d.p("Microalgae could diversify the feedstock base, but a hydrocarbon fuel made by hydrothermal liquefaction (HTL) is not an ester, so cost parity with CME would not by itself make it eligible for the mandate; in this study CME is an economic benchmark and eligibility would require a separate regulatory determination. In principle they grow on non-arable land and waste water and can use CO₂ from flue gas [@chisti2007,quinn2015]. Harmonised designs by the US national laboratories put mature-scale fuel costs at about 4–5 USD per gallon gasoline-equivalent at 25 g/m²/d [@jones2014,davis2014], while annual outdoor productivities measured at scale remain between about 10 and 21 g/m²/d [@klein2024,huntley2015,jo2020]. Life-cycle studies show that the climate benefit depends strongly on the electricity used to mix ponds and move water [@pegallapati2015,frank2013,bennion2015]. Assessments for tropical developing countries, where both grids and costs differ, are scarce.")
d.p("A plant to grow *C. vulgaris* on the flue gas and wastewater of San Carlos BioPower, Negros Occidental, was designed in 2018 [@cereno2018] and evaluated in 2021 with a multi-attribute model that ranked its best variant at a net present value of PHP 1.04 billion [@aviso2021]; a draft working paper prepared for a December 2026 winter school re-analysed the cash flows and found a levelised cost of about 0.8 USD per litre [@cereno2026]. None of these studies recomputed the material balance. Here we do so, build a harmonised TEA–LCA engine around a corrected design, and extend the earlier work in five ways:")
d.nums(["the inherited process balance is verified from first principles and bridged quantitatively to a corrected design;",
        "one balance drives both the TEA and an LCA on ecoinvent 3.12, with the Visayas grid taken from a DOE-calibrated Philippine grid model [@evidente2026grid];",
        "uncertainty is propagated with correlated Monte Carlo sampling, Sobol' indices and stochastic cash flows that include typhoon outages, and prospective pathways run to 2050;",
        "NSGA-II Pareto fronts of cost, climate and capital are computed for 2026, 2040 and 2050;",
        "a machine-learning surrogate is trained, validated and deployed as a web calculator."])
d.p("The 2026 closure of the Strait of Hormuz, which raised Philippine fuel, LPG and fertiliser prices sharply, sets the price context of the assessment [@evidente2026grid,tribune2026a,wb2026].")

d.h1("2. Materials and methods", bm="ms_methods")
d.h2("2.1. Goal, scope and system boundary")
d.p(f"The plant adjoins San Carlos BioPower, whose biogenic flue gas enters burden-free [@unfccc2012]. The base design has {f0(H['area_ha'])} ha of unlined paddlewheel raceways, flue-gas sparging and a settler–membrane–centrifuge harvest to 20% solids, after the NREL farm design [@davis2016,klein2024]. Route A liquefies the whole biomass at 350 °C and 20.9 MPa and hydrotreats the biocrude to renewable diesel and naphtha, recycling the aqueous phase as a nutrient source [@jones2014,zhu2023]. Route B extracts lipids with hexane, transesterifies the triglyceride fraction to FAME and digests the residue, together with the non-transesterifiable extract, in a biogas CHP plant [@davis2012,pradhan2012]. The functional unit is 1 MJ of fuel (LHV) at the plant gate; costs are also given per litre of diesel-equivalent (35.9 MJ). The boundary is cradle-to-gate plus delivery to a regional depot and combustion CH₄ and N₂O; retail distribution is excluded, and the 94 g CO₂e/MJ fossil comparator is the RED II regulatory value, not a Philippine diesel inventory [@red2] («fig:F01a_system_boundary»); «fig:F01b_pfd_htl» shows the HTL flowsheet.".replace("{fig:", "«fig:").replace("_boundary}", "_boundary»").replace("_htl}", "_htl»"))
d.fig("F01a_system_boundary"); d.fig("F01b_pfd_htl")
d.h2("2.2. Process model and parameter registry")
d.p(f"An annual steady-state balance computes biomass growth (productivity × area × on-stream factor × typhoon availability), CO₂ demand from a fixation of {B['co2_fix']} kg CO₂/kg with {pct(B['co2_util'])} utilisation, nutrient demand, HTL heat duty with {pct(B['htl_hr'])} feed–effluent recovery, biocrude ({B['htl_y']} kg/kg AFDW) and hydrogen use ({B['ht_h2']} kg/kg biocrude), lipid extraction and transesterification, and all utilities [@davis2016,jones2014,zheng2016,chisti2007]. All {NPAR} inputs sit in a registry with base value, range, distribution, sources and a confidence rating from A (design data for the same process) to D (unsourced assumption) [@weidema1996]; {CONF['A']} are rated A, {CONF['B']} B, {CONF['C']} C and {CONF['D']} D. «tab:ms_par» lists the most influential. Biomass grown, CO₂ delivered and the HTL heat duty are")
d.eq("m_{grown} = 0.01 · P · A · 365 · a · (1 − λd/365)", 1)
d.eq("m_{CO2} = 1.83 · m_{grown} / u_{CO2}", 2)
d.eq("Q_{HTL} = (m_{dry} / x_{s}) · c_{p} · ΔT · (1 − ε)", 3)
d.p("where m_{grown} is in t/yr, P is the areal productivity (g/m²/d), A the pond area (ha; 0.01 converts g·ha to t), a the on-stream factor, λ the rate of damaging typhoons and d the production days lost per event, u_{CO2} the fraction of delivered CO₂ taken up, x_{s} the slurry total dry solids, c_{p} = 3.8 kJ/kg/K, ΔT = 320 K and ε the feed–effluent heat recovery.")
keys = ["prod", "htl_y", "ht_h2", "ht_y", "mix_kwh", "co2_kwh", "dew_kwh", "fame_frac", "lipid", "ef_grid", "p_el", "p_h2", "cap_farm_acre", "loc_f", "r"]
rows = [[REG.P[k]["name"], f"{REG.P[k]['base']:g}", f"{REG.P[k]['low']:g}–{REG.P[k]['high']:g}", REG.P[k]["unit"], REG.P[k]["conf"], [{"citek": REG.P[k]["src"]}]] for k in keys]
d.b.append({"k": "table", "tid": "ms_par", "bm": "tab_ms_par", "cap": ["Key parameters (complete registry in the Supplementary database)."], "head": [[h] for h in ["Parameter", "Base", "Range", "Unit", "Conf.", "Sources"]],
            "rows": [[(c if isinstance(c, list) else [str(c)]) for c in r] for r in rows], "widths": [2.6, 0.8, 1.1, 1.3, 0.5, 1.4], "align": ["l", "r", "r", "l", "l", "l"], "note": None, "size": 15})
d.p("The complete set of 33 model equations, covering cultivation, conversion, utilities, capital, cash flow, life cycle impacts, prospective pathways, uncertainty, sensitivity, stochastic cash flows, optimisation and the surrogates, is given in Appendix A.")
d.h2("2.3. Techno-economic and life cycle models")
d.p(f"Capital is scaled bottom-up from the NREL and PNNL equipment lists with capacity exponents, escalated with CEPCI to 2025 (813) and a Southeast Asian proxy location factor of {B['loc_f']}, giving an AACE Class 4 estimate [@klein2024,jones2014,towler2022,aace2020]. Installed cost is converted to fixed capital with {B['tci_conv']} for conversion (PNNL: (467.9 − 22.3 working capital)/252.6) and {B['tci_farm']} for the farm (NREL total-to-installed 1.64 without its 5% working capital), and working capital of 5% is added once. Capital is in USD 2025 and operating costs at 2026 prices, without escalation between the two. Operating costs use September–October 2026 Philippine prices, including an estimated industrial electricity tariff of {f2(B['p_el'])} USD/kWh [@negrosnow2026,wb2026]. A real, unlevered 30-year discounted cash flow at {pct(B['r'])} with an assumed six-year income-tax holiday (conditional on incentive registration), 20% corporate tax and tax losses carried forward by vintage for five years (losses incurred during the holiday are not carried over) [@ra8424,firb2025] gives the minimum fuel selling price (MFSP) as the constant real price p at which [@davis2016,gt2025]")
d.eq("Σ_{t} [p·Q_{t} + R_{t} − C_{t} − T_{t}(p) − I_{t}] · (1 + r)^{−t} = 0", 4)
d.p("with Q_{t} fuel output, R_{t} co-product revenue, C_{t} operating cost, T_{t} tax, I_{t} investment and r = 10%. Foreground flows are linked to 26,533 ecoinvent 3.12 cut-off activities and characterised with IPCC 2021 GWP100 and the EF 3.0 method (adapted) [@ecoinvent312,forster2021,fazio2018]; electricity is the Visayas low-voltage mix of our companion Philippine grid model (1.137 kg CO₂e/kWh in 2024; under review), a conservative proxy for the higher-voltage supply priced in the TEA [@evidente2026grid]. Hydrogen (generic ecoinvent market, while the TEA prices LPG reforming) and process heat (LPG-related GWP with the other indicators of a natural-gas furnace) are proxies, tested in the sensitivity analysis. Impacts per MJ are h = U·f / E_{fuel}, where U holds the cradle-to-gate unit scores of the background activities, f the foreground demand vector and E_{fuel} the fuel energy [@heijungs2002]. Co-products are handled by one whole-system energy-allocation factor, following the principle of RED II but not its process-step and cogeneration rules, so GHG savings are indicative rather than a compliance calculation; system expansion is a sensitivity [@red2].")
d.h2("2.4. Verification and validation")
d.p(f"{NVER} quantities of the inherited balance and financial model were recomputed from stoichiometry, thermodynamics, published productivity and the original workbooks, each traced to a workbook cell or thesis page. The engine passes {NCHK} implementation and consistency checks (definitions, recycle bounds, cash-flow and impact closure); these do not amount to an element-by-element mass and energy closure. It was validated by running it in the configuration of three published studies [@klein2024,jones2014,pegallapati2015].")
d.h2("2.5. Scenarios, sensitivity and prospective pathways")
d.p("Sixty scenarios cover design variants, biology, energy supply, policy and finance, prospective years and accounting choices; each is recalculated in full. One-at-a-time sensitivity uses the registry ranges. Break-even values are found by root-finding on the engine. Prospective pathways to 2050 combine the grid emission factors of the Philippine Energy Plan 2023–2050 scenarios and a net-zero case [@pep2023,evidente2026grid] with trajectories for productivity, HTL yield, hydrogen and fertiliser intensity, and capital learning at 13% per doubling [@hettinga2009,thonemann2020].")
d.h2("2.6. Uncertainty, dependency, risk and global sensitivity")
d.p(f"Monte Carlo simulation used {NMC:,} Latin-hypercube draws of all {len(UNC['keys'])} ranged parameters, applied to six designs as common random numbers [@igos2019,heijungs2020]. Sixteen rank correlations among prices, capital factors and biomass composition were imposed with the Iman–Conover method [@iman1982]. Risk is summarised by probabilities of meeting cost and climate targets and by the value at risk of NPV. A separate stochastic cash-flow model adds mean-reverting log fuel prices [@schwartz1997], Poisson typhoon outages and interannual productivity noise [@cinco2016,mcgowen2023]. Global sensitivity used Morris screening of all parameters [@morris1991] and Sobol' indices, with independently sampled inputs, for the 30 most important, with {SOB['HTL']['n']:,} base samples for HTL ({SOB['HTL']['runs']:,} runs) and bootstrap confidence intervals [@saltelli2010,sobol2001]. The first-order and total-order indices of input X_{{i}} are")
d.eq("S_{i} = V[E(Y | X_{i})] / V(Y),     S_{Ti} = E[V(Y | X_{~i})] / V(Y)", 5)
d.p("estimated with the Saltelli and Jansen estimators [@saltelli2010].")
d.h2("2.7. Multi-objective optimisation and surrogate models")
d.p(f"NSGA-II [@deb2002] (population 100, 80 generations) minimised MFSP, GWP and total capital investment over route, pond area (50–3,000 ha) and eight discrete design choices, for the 2026, 2040 (CES2) and 2050 (net-zero) backgrounds. Surrogates of MFSP, GWP, capital and output were trained on {ML['HTL']['n']:,} engine runs per route over 17 inputs: quadratic ridge regression, random forest, gradient boosting, a 64–64 multilayer perceptron and a Gaussian process [@pedregosa2011,breiman2001,friedman2001]. They were validated on a 20% hold-out, five-fold cross-validation, learning curves and an extrapolation test.")

d.h1("3. Results", bm="ms_results")
d.h2("3.1. Verification of the inherited design")
d.p(f"{NVFAIL} of {NVER} checks fail, {sum(1 for c in VER if c['verdict'] == 'Fail' and c['severity'] == 'High')} with high severity. The balance feeds 79.5 t/d of algae into the plant as starting culture and grows 0.5 t/d; producing 80 t/d on the 10.36 ha of ponds would require 772 g/m²/d, about 40 times the measured range. The HTL reactor has no heat duty although heating the slurry needs 7.5 MW, its tri- and diglyceride products amount to 174% of the lipid fed (impossible, since these esters form only from lipid), the workbooks contain broken #REF! references, and nitrogen and CO₂ supplies cover 0.6% and 0.45% of demand. «fig:F04_bridge» bridges the reported cost to the corrected design holding 24,000 t/yr of biomass entering conversion: the engine gives {f2(BR[1]['mfsp'])} USD/L deq on the original basis, of the same order as the reported value, and bottom-up capital and growing the biomass raise it to {f2(BR[3]['mfsp'])}; HTL heat, hydrotreating and nutrients bring it to {f2(BR[6]['mfsp'])}. The engine passes all its consistency checks and reproduces the NREL farm (unlined case), PNNL HTL and Argonne LCA results within {100*(VAL[0]['model']/VAL[0]['published']-1):+.0f}%, {100*(VAL[1]['model']/VAL[1]['published']-1):+.0f}% and {100*(VAL[2]['model']/VAL[2]['published']-1):+.0f}%.".replace("«fig:F04_bridge»", "«fig:F04_bridge»"))
d.fig("F04_bridge")
d.h2("3.2. Base designs")
rows = [["Pond area, ha", f0(H["area_ha"]), f0(F["area_ha"])], ["Biomass harvested, t AFDW/yr", f0(H["afdw"]), f0(F["afdw"])], ["Fuel output, ML diesel-eq./yr", f2(H["ldeq"] / 1e6), f2(F["ldeq"] / 1e6)],
        ["Electricity demand, GWh/yr", f1(H["el_dem"] / 1e6), f1(F["el_dem"] / 1e6)], ["Purchased process heat, TJ/yr (FAME: CHP surplus)", f1(H["heat_net"] / 1e6), f1(F["heat_net"] / 1e6)], ["Total capital investment, USD M", f0(H["tci"] / 1e6), f0(F["tci"] / 1e6)],
        ["Operating cost, USD M/yr", f1(H["opex"] / 1e6), f1(F["opex"] / 1e6)], ["MFSP, USD/L diesel-eq.", f2(H["mfsp_ldeq"]), f2(F["mfsp_ldeq"])], ["MFSP, USD/GJ", f0(H["mfsp_gj"]), f0(F["mfsp_gj"])],
        ["NPV at the CME price, USD M", f0(H["npv"] / 1e6), f0(F["npv"] / 1e6)], ["GWP, g CO₂e/MJ", f1(H["gwp"]), f1(F["gwp"])], ["GHG saving vs 94 g/MJ", f"{H['saving']*100:.1f}%", pct(F["saving"], 1)],
        ["Land occupation incl. 25% allowance, m²·yr/GJ", f1(H["land_m2_per_GJ"]), f1(F["land_m2_per_GJ"])], ["Net water, L/MJ", f1(H["water_L_per_MJ"]), f1(F["water_L_per_MJ"])]]
d.table("ms_base", "Base designs (2026, 400 ha of ponds, Visayas grid). Land occupation includes a 25% allowance for roads, harvest and conversion units (500 ha in total).", ["Indicator", "HTL", "FAME"], rows, widths=[3, 1.2, 1.2], align=["l", "r", "r"])
c = H["contrib"]
d.p(f"The HTL plant makes {f2(H['ldeq']/1e6)} million litres diesel-equivalent per year from {f0(H['afdw'])} t of biomass for {f0(H['tci']/1e6)} M USD of capital («tab:ms_base»). Its MFSP of {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq is {f1(H['mfsp_ldeq']/CME)} times the CME price and {f1(H['mfsp_ldeq']/DSL)} times diesel before tax. FAME converts {pct(F['fuel_mj']/(F['afdw']*22000))} of the biomass energy into fuel against {pct(H['fuel_mj']/(H['afdw']*22000))} for HTL and costs three times as much. The HTL GWP of {f1(H['gwp'])} g/MJ is dominated by electricity ({f1(c['Electricity'])} g/MJ), nutrients ({f1(c['Nutrients'])}) and hydrogen ({f1(c['Hydrogen'])}) («fig:F11_gwp_contrib»); FAME emits {f1(F['gwp'])} g/MJ, mainly from nutrients and digester methane slip.".replace("«tab:ms_base»", "«tab:ms_base»").replace("«fig:F11_gwp_contrib»", "«fig:F11_gwp_contrib»"))
d.fig("F11_gwp_contrib")
d.h2("3.3. Scenarios and break-even")
sel = ["S01", "S02", "S03", "S07", "S12", "S15", "S10", "S21", "S23", "S27", "S28", "S32", "S34", "S35", "S41", "S42", "S50", "S54", "S56", "S58"]
rows = [[s, SDEF[s]["name"], f2(S[s]["mfsp_ldeq"]), f1(S[s]["gwp"]), f0(S[s]["tci"] / 1e6)] for s in sel]
d.table("ms_scen", "Selected scenarios (all 60 in the Supplementary database).", ["ID", "Scenario", "MFSP, USD/L deq", "GWP, g/MJ", "TCI, USD M"], rows, widths=[0.5, 4, 1, 0.9, 0.8], align=["l", "l", "r", "r", "r"], size=15)
d.p(f"Scale lowers the HTL MFSP to {f2(S['S15']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq at about 2,000 ha (2,023 ha modelled); full pond liners raise it to {f2(S['S03']['mfsp_ldeq'])} («tab:ms_scen», «fig:F14_scenario_map»). Electricity supply decides the climate result: a hypothetical PPA with the bagasse power plant at the Green Energy Auction biomass tariff, whose availability is not established, gives {f1(S['S27']['gwp'])} g/MJ at slightly lower cost, and adding biomass heat and low-carbon hydrogen reaches {f1(S['S32']['gwp'])} g/MJ ({pct(S['S32']['saving'])} saving). Purchased liquid CO₂ ({f1(S['S10']['gwp'])} g/MJ) or a fossil CO₂ source ({f1(S['S58']['gwp'])}) would erase the benefit. Productivity, HTL yield, discount rate and location factor lead the tornado for cost («fig:F15_tornado_htl_mfsp»). The CME break-even needs {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL)'))} g/m²/d at 400 ha and {f0(be('Productivity for MFSP = CME price (HTL, 2,000 ha)'))} at about 2,000 ha («fig:F20_breakeven_prod_area»), or capital at {pct(be('Capital multiplier for MFSP = CME (HTL)'))} of the estimate; HTL is cheaper than FAME unless lipid exceeds {pct(be('Lipid content for FAME MFSP = HTL MFSP (crossover)'))}.".replace("«tab:ms_scen»", "«tab:ms_scen»").replace("«fig:F14_scenario_map»", "«fig:F14_scenario_map»").replace("«fig:F15_tornado_htl_mfsp»", "«fig:F15_tornado_htl_mfsp»").replace("«fig:F20_breakeven_prod_area»", "«fig:F20_breakeven_prod_area»"))
d.fig("F14_scenario_map"); d.fig("F15_tornado_htl_mfsp"); d.fig("F20_breakeven_prod_area")
d.h2("3.4. Prospective results")
d.p(f"Along the Reference pathway the HTL MFSP falls to {f2(S['S45']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq by 2050 and remains above CME («fig:F22_prospective»). Under CES1 it falls below the fixed CME benchmark in {XOVER[('HTL','CES1')]}; under CES2 in {XOVER[('HTL','CES2')]}, reaching {f2(S['S51']['mfsp_ldeq'])} in 2050 with a {pct(S['S51']['saving'])} GHG saving; under the net-zero pathway in {XOVER[('HTL','NZ')]}, reaching {f2(S['S54']['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD/L deq and {f1(S['S54']['gwp'])} g/MJ in 2050. These trajectories assume productivity of 27–30 g/m²/d and four to six capacity doublings by 2050.".replace("«fig:F22_prospective»", "«fig:F22_prospective»"))
d.fig("F22_prospective")
d.h2("3.5. Uncertainty, dependency and risk")
rows = [[k, f2(r["mfsp_p50"]), f"{f2(r['mfsp_p5'])}–{f2(r['mfsp_p95'])}", f1(r["gwp_p50"]), pct(r["p_lt_cme"]), pct(r["p_lt_cme_sampled"]), pct(r["p_save50"]), f0(r["var5"] / 1e6)] for k, r in RISK.items()]
d.table("ms_risk", f"Monte Carlo results and risk metrics ({NMC:,} draws). CME fixed: the 2026 benchmark of {f2(CME)} USD/L deq; CME sampled: the CME price drawn in the same draw, equivalent to P(NPV > 0).", ["Design", "MFSP P50", "P5–P95", "GWP P50", "P(MFSP<CME fixed)", "P(MFSP<CME sampled)", "P(save ≥50%)", "VaR₅, USD M"], rows, widths=[2, 0.75, 1.05, 0.75, 0.95, 0.95, 0.85, 0.75], align=["l"] + ["r"] * 7, size=14)
d.p(f"None of the evaluated 2026 designs undercuts the CME benchmark in more than 1 of 10,000 draws («tab:ms_risk», «fig:F27_mc_joint»). The HTL base has a 90% MFSP interval of {f2(r1['mfsp_p5'])}–{f2(r1['mfsp_p95'])} USD/L deq and a median GWP of {f1(r1['gwp_p50'])} g/MJ, above the deterministic value because the electricity-related ranges are right-skewed; it emits less than fossil diesel in only {pct(r1['p_below_diesel'])} of draws. In 2050 under the net-zero pathway P(MFSP < CME) is {pct(r54['p_lt_cme'])} against the fixed benchmark and {pct(r54['p_lt_cme_sampled'])} against the sampled price; these are probabilities conditional on the modelled pathway, not forecasts. Input dependencies change the MFSP distribution little; HTL beats FAME on cost in {pct(UNC['p_htl_beats_fame']['mfsp'])} of draws and on GWP in {pct(UNC['p_htl_beats_fame']['gwp'])}. In the stochastic cash-flow model, typhoon outages cost about {f0((ST['S01 no typhoons']['median']-ST['S01 HTL base']['median'])/1e6)} M USD of median NPV; for the 2050 design paid the CME price, {ST_TXT}, while at the diesel price it loses money in {pct(ST['S54 sold at diesel parity']['p_loss'])} of paths.".replace("«tab:ms_risk»", "«tab:ms_risk»").replace("«fig:F27_mc_joint»", "«fig:F27_mc_joint»"))
d.fig("F27_mc_joint")
d.h2("3.6. Global sensitivity")
h1 = sob_top("HTL", 1)
d.p(f"With independently sampled inputs, the first-order Sobol' indices of the HTL MFSP are {h0[0][1]:.2f} for productivity and {h0[1][1]:.2f} for HTL yield (total order {h0[0][2]:.2f} and {h0[1][2]:.2f}), followed by the discount rate (ST {h0[2][2]:.2f}) and the location factor ({h0[3][2]:.2f}) («fig:F34_sobol_htl»). These indices describe the model under independent inputs, not the correlated Monte Carlo distribution. For GWP, HTL yield ({h1[0][2]:.2f}), CO₂ delivery energy ({h1[1][2]:.2f}), mixing energy ({h1[2][2]:.2f}) and productivity ({h1[3][2]:.2f}) lead. First-order indices sum to {SOB['HTL']['S1'][:,0].sum():.2f} and {SOB['HTL']['S1'][:,1].sum():.2f}, so interactions are minor.".replace("«fig:F34_sobol_htl»", "«fig:F34_sobol_htl»"))
d.fig("F34_sobol_htl")
d.h2("3.7. Pareto fronts")
d.p(f"Within the optimisation domain, which does not constrain CO₂ supply, the cheapest Pareto-optimal designs are HTL plants at the 3,000 ha bound with biomass heat, wastewater nutrients and a low-carbon PPA (biomass in 2026, solar in 2050): {f2(a26['mfsp'])} USD/L deq and {f1(a26['gwp'])} g/MJ in 2026, and {f2(a50['mfsp'])} USD/L deq and {f1(a50['gwp'])} g/MJ in 2050 («fig:F37_pareto»). Lower GWP is bought with CHG treatment of the aqueous phase and smaller plants whose nitrogen comes largely from municipal wastewater. The flue gas of the 18 MW power plant can supply roughly 1,150 ha of ponds, so these optima, and the 2,000 ha scenarios, assume a second free source of biogenic CO₂; purchased liquid CO₂ would add about 1 USD/L deq.".replace("«fig:F37_pareto»", "«fig:F37_pareto»"))
d.fig("F37_pareto")
d.h2("3.8. Surrogate models")
d.p(f"The multilayer perceptron reproduces the engine with hold-out R² of {mh['mfsp_ldeq']['r2']:.4f} (MFSP) and {mh['gwp']['r2']:.4f} (GWP) and mean absolute percentage errors of {pct(mh['mfsp_ldeq']['mape'],1)} and {pct(mh['gwp']['mape'],1)} for HTL ({pct(mh['tci']['mape'],1)} for capital and {pct(mh['ldeq']['mape'],1)} for output); these measure agreement with the engine, not with a real plant; five-fold cross-validation gives R² ≥ {ML['HTL']['cv']['Neural network (MLP 64-64)'].min():.4f} («fig:F41_parity»). The Gaussian process is more accurate but less portable; random forests and quadratic models are not accurate enough. In a separate experiment, a model trained only below 25 g/m²/d had a MAPE of {pct(ML['HTL']['extrap']['Neural network (MLP 64-64)']['mape'],1)} above 30 g/m²/d; the deployed model is trained over 8–40 g/m²/d. The calculator computes annual output analytically rather than from the network, because the network responds slightly to inputs with no physical effect on output (approximation error).".replace("«fig:F41_parity»", "«fig:F41_parity»"))
d.fig("F41_parity")

d.h1("4. Discussion", bm="ms_disc")
d.p(f"The central finding is that the earlier, favourable assessments of this plant rested on a balance in which the algae were free. Once the biomass is grown at a realistic productivity, the plant needs about {f0(CORE['area80']/10.36)} times more pond area, about twenty times more capital, a heated HTL reactor, an upgrading step and purchased nutrients, and its cost rises about {f1(BR[-1]['mfsp']/BR[0]['mfsp'])}-fold. The ranking of the 2021 variants survives, but not their profitability, and the conclusion of our draft working paper that microalgae fuel could be cheaper than CME is reversed [@aviso2021,cereno2026]. Recomputing inherited balances from first principles, routinely done in process design, should be part of any re-assessment of published TEA results.")
d.p(f"Two features of the Philippine setting shape the results. First, the Visayas grid emits about 2.6 times as much CO₂ per kWh as the US grid assumed in most algae LCAs, so open-pond electricity cancels the climate benefit [@pegallapati2015,evidente2026grid]; a PPA with the adjacent bagasse plant would restore it at no cost penalty in the model because the auction tariff undercuts the grid generation charge [@solarquarter2023,malaya2024rcoa], although proximity and a tariff do not establish that such a contract would be offered. Second, Philippine capital and utility costs and the modest scale available at one power plant keep the MFSP well above the 1.9 USD/L deq implied by the PNNL design case [@jones2014]. Productivity and yield, not prices or wages, explain most of the variance; the research priority is biology, and a multi-year outdoor pilot at the site would be the decisive next step.")
d.p("Limitations include the Class 4 capital estimate scaled from US designs with a regional proxy location factor, an estimated (not quoted) electricity tariff, yield-based HTL and hydrotreating models without element-by-element closure, proxy inventories for hydrogen and process heat, a PV-only inventory for the solar-plus-storage scenario, a grid factor from a companion model still under review, six unsourced assumptions in the base case (chiefly land lease, typhoon frequency and outage length), a static background database apart from electricity, hydrogen and fertiliser, and the exclusion of indirect land-use change. Eligibility of HTL fuel under the Biofuels Act is not assessed. The prospective results depend on learning across a global industry that a single Philippine plant would not drive.")
d.h1("5. Conclusions", bm="ms_concl")
d.p(f"Corrected for growing its own biomass, a flue-gas microalgae fuel plant in Negros Occidental would cost {f2(H['mfsp_ldeq'])} USD per litre diesel-equivalent in 2026, about twice the price of coconut methyl ester, and on the current grid would give negligible greenhouse-gas savings. Low-carbon electricity is the condition for any climate benefit, and a supply contract with the neighbouring biomass plant would need to be confirmed; productivity and biocrude yield are the conditions for competitiveness and are not yet demonstrated. With grid decarbonisation, learning and biological progress, the modelled cost falls below the CME benchmark in {XOVER[('HTL','NZ')]} on a net-zero pathway, {XOVER[('HTL','CES2')]} and {XOVER[('HTL','CES1')]} on the clean-energy pathways, and not by 2050 on the Reference pathway. Microalgae are therefore at most a long-term option for diversifying Philippine biodiesel-blend feedstock, and whether HTL fuel could count toward the mandate would require a separate regulatory determination.")
